<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/8th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saxon Wars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">772–<a href="/years/804">804</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> Wars were the campaigns fought from 772 to 804 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> against the Saxons, a Germanic people living east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. The conflict involved repeated annual campaigns, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">mass deportations</a> and the forced Christianisation of the Saxons. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a> present the wars as showing both the strengths and limits of Carolingian military power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saxon-wars.jpg" alt="Saxon Wars" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sémhur · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frankish_Empire_481_to_814-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> were divided into four groups in four regions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> lay nearest the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Frankish kingdom</a> of Austrasia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastphalia">Eastphalia</a> farthest, with Engria (or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angria">Engern</a>) between them and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nordalbingia">Nordalbingia</a> to the north, at the base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> peninsula. The first war began in January 772, when a Saxon expedition sacked and burned the church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deventer">Deventer</a>. Charlemagne then invaded Saxon territory, subjugated the Engrians and destroyed their sacred symbol, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irminsul">Irminsul</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paderborn">Paderborn</a>. Irminsul may have been a hollow tree trunk representing the pillar that supported the skies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In all, 18 campaigns were fought, mostly in what is now northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Charlemagne's campaigns in 775 and 776 brought all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nordalbingia">Nordalbingia</a> under his control, but rebellions destroyed his fortress at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eresburg">Eresburg</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind">Widukind</a> fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a>. In 777 a diet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paderborn">Paderborn</a> set out to bring Saxony into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Frankish kingdom</a>. Many Saxons were baptised and missionaries, mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxons</a>, were recruited. Charlemagne issued decrees that imposed capital punishment for observing heathen practices, which earned him the title butcher of the Saxons. His adviser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcuin-of-york">Alcuin of York</a> urged leniency.</p><p>In 782 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind">Widukind</a> returned and led a revolt, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> won the Battle of Süntel. In response <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> ordered the beheading of 4,500 Saxons at the Massacre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verden">Verden</a>. Warfare was constant in 783–785, and in 785 Widukind was baptised and swore fealty. Revolts continued sporadically.</p><p>Westphalians rose in 792 against forcible recruitment for the wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a>, and Eastphalians and Nordalbingians joined in 793, but it was put down by 794. In 798 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obotrites">Obotrites</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drasco">Thrasco</a> defeated the Nordalbingian Saxons at Bornhöved, killing between 2,800 and 4,000. In 804 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> deported 10,000 Nordalbingians to Neustria and gave their lands to the king of the Obotrites.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/einhard">Einhard</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s biographer, said the war ended when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> accepted terms: they renounced their religious customs, accepted the sacraments of the Christian faith and united with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> as one people. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulary">capitulary</a> of 785, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulatio-de-partibus-saxoniae">Capitulatio de partibus Saxoniae</a>, had prescribed death for any Saxon who remained unbaptised and pagan, and a poet of the Paderborn Epic praised terror as a means of conversion.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcuin-of-york">Alcuin</a> argued that faith is a free act of the will and that people could be forced to be baptised but not to believe. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> abolished the death penalty for paganism in 797 and in 802 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> common law was codified as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lex-saxonum">Lex Saxonum</a>. Bishoprics were founded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paderborn">Paderborn</a>, Münster, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minden">Minden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verden">Verden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osnabruck">Osnabrück</a>. A last Saxon uprising, the Stellinga, took place between 841 and 845.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saxon Wars” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Saxon Wars</b> (772–804) subjected the pagan Saxons east of the Rhine through more than thirty years of brutal campaigning, mass deportations and forced Christianisation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Saxons lost their independence to Charlemagne in the long <b>Saxon Wars</b> of 772–804.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saxons">Saxons · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
